Lab 3.2, Exercise 2
a. di runs its first argument as a command ($1 gets replaced with
   the argument text, then executed).
   Command: ./di 'ls -l'
   The quotes keep "ls -l" as one argument. Without them, $1 would
   just be ls.
b. Prediction:  a 1 aC 10
   Actual:      a 1 a1C 1*10
   - C: the braces just mark where the variable name ends, so
     ${B}C becomes 1C, giving a1C.
   - D: bash doesn't do math in assignments. Everything is a
     string, so D is literally 1*10.
